    "text": "Alkylation of Acetylide Anions"
  },
  "documentClass": null,
  "metadata": {
    "uuid": "85ac5976-44f9-450e-a3eb-3d3ca3d2d955"
  },
  "content": [
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00001",
      "segmentId": "m00110:para:para-00001"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00001",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00110:alt:fig-00001-alt",
          "text": "The figure shows the reaction mechanism of an acetylide anion with methyl bromide to form propyne and sodium bromide."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_09_08_001.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00002",
      "segmentId": "m00110:para:para-00002"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00002",
      "class": "mechanism-figure scaled-down",
      "caption": {
        "segmentId": "m00110:caption:fig-00002-caption"
      },
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00110:alt:fig-00002-alt",
          "text": "The figure shows a two-step mechanism of an acetylide anion reacting with bromomethane to form propyne. The reaction proceeds through a transition state."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_09_08_002.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00003",
      "segmentId": "m00110:para:para-00003"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00003",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00110:alt:fig-00003-alt",
          "text": "The reaction shows 1-hexyne reacting with sodium amide, ammonia and bromobutane to form 5-decyne (76%)."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_09_08_003.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00004",
      "segmentId": "m00110:para:para-00004"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00004",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00110:alt:fig-00004-alt",
          "text": "Figure shows the alkylation of terminal alkynes with the same reagents. The first reaction forms a terminal alkyne. The second reaction forms an internal alkyne."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_09_08_004.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00005",
      "segmentId": "m00110:para:para-00005"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00005",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00110:alt:fig-00005-alt",
          "text": "Bromocyclohexane (a secondary alkyl halide) reacts with sodium acetylide anion to form cyclohexene, propyne, and sodium bromide. 1-cyclohexyl-1-propyne is not formed."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_09_08_005.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
